# These entries describe *specific technical ideas* rather than a universal filler paragraph.
# key: anchors in titles; explanation, example, constraint, check, question, correct, distractors
PROFILES=[
 ('SHACL SPARQL', ['shacl sparql','sparql-based constraint'], 'A SHACL-SPARQL constraint expresses validation logic as a SELECT query evaluated against focus nodes. It complements standard SHACL Core components when a rule needs a join, aggregate, or other graph pattern.', 'A publication shape could report a study as invalid when its source dataset has been retired but the study still advertises the dataset as active.', 'Constraint queries need careful variable binding, bounded complexity, and explicit error reporting; avoid using custom SPARQL for checks that SHACL Core already expresses.', 'Run a deliberately failing fixture and confirm the validation report identifies the focus node, message, severity, and offending path.', 'What does a SHACL-SPARQL constraint return for each invalid focus node?', 'Validation results generated from a SELECT pattern', ['Only inferred subclass axioms','A replacement for RDF identifiers']),
 ('SHACL', ['shacl','node shape','property shape','validation report','minimum count','maximum count','datatype constraint','node-kind constraint','class constraint','value-range constraint','qualified value shape','closed shape'], 'SHACL describes expectations of RDF data as shapes. Targets choose focus nodes, property shapes follow predicates, and components such as sh:minCount, sh:datatype, and sh:class state what a conforming node needs.', 'A catalog contract can require every dcat:Dataset to have one title and a responsible publisher, then produce a concrete validation report when a source fails.', 'Do not confuse SHACL conformance with OWL reasoning. OWL may infer new facts under open-world semantics; a SHACL check can still report that required supplied data is missing.', 'Test valid, missing, wrong datatype, duplicated and unexpected-value fixtures. Check the results graph, not just the true/false flag.', 'Which technology is primarily designed to report RDF data constraint violations?', 'SHACL', ['RDFS domain inference','OWL functional property']),
 ('SPARQL', ['sparql','select','filter','group by','having','order by','optional','union','property path','construct','ask quer','service quer','bind','values','distinct','limit and offset','multi-pattern join','basic graph pattern','query planning','query optimization','variable projection'], 'SPARQL evaluates graph patterns against RDF data. Basic graph patterns join shared variables; modifiers shape results, and different query forms such as SELECT, ASK and CONSTRUCT return different kinds of output.', 'Find datasets whose steward belongs to a specific organization by joining ?dataset ex:steward ?person and ?person ex:memberOf ?org; keep variables shared across the two patterns.', 'OPTIONAL can introduce unbound variables; filters and joins can alter result multiplicity. LIMIT without an explicit ORDER BY does not give reproducible pagination.', 'Write a positive and a negative fixture, inspect row counts, and compare the results against a known answer table. Explain why each selected variable is bound.', 'What connects multiple SPARQL patterns into a join?', 'Shared variables across triple patterns', ['Visual alignment of diagram nodes','Having the same textual labels']),
 ('SKOS', ['skos','thesaur','taxonomy','controlled vocab','preferred label','alternative label','hidden label','broader','narrower','exact match','close match','broad match','term lists','concept scheme','code list','polyhierarch','reference value','terminology'], 'SKOS models knowledge-organization systems. A skos:Concept is identified independently of its human-readable labels. skos:prefLabel is a preferred lexical form, skos:altLabel captures alternatives, and skos:broader expresses a semantic hierarchy.', 'A governed party-status scheme can preserve ex:Active as a stable concept IRI while different applications use labels such as Active, Enabled, or Current.', 'SKOS broader relations are not identical to OWL subclass relations. A term taxonomy is not automatically a logical classification of instance-bearing OWL classes.', 'Verify each concept has a resolvable identifier, language-specific label policy, valid scheme membership, and explicit review for exact or close mappings.', 'What should stay stable when a SKOS preferred label changes?', 'The concept identifier (IRI)', ['All display labels must be frozen','The diagram location']),
 ('RDFS', ['rdfs','subclass','subclassof','subproperty','domain inference','range inference','rdf:type semantics','rdfs:class','classes & properties','class hierarchy'], 'RDFS adds schema meaning to RDF with classes, properties, domains, ranges, subclass and subproperty links. These axioms support entailments; they are not data-entry forms or closed-world validation rules.', 'If ex:ResearchStudy rdfs:subClassOf ex:ResearchAsset, then a study typed as ex:ResearchStudy is also an instance of ex:ResearchAsset under RDFS entailment.', 'A property domain or range can infer a class for a subject or object; it does not prohibit the property from appearing on an unexpected individual.', 'Compare asserted triples with the closure of inferred triples, and test unexpected domain use to understand what inference actually produces.', 'What does rdfs:subClassOf make possible?', 'Type inference from a subclass to a superclass', ['Deletion of untyped nodes','Automatic cardinality validation']),
 ('OWL', ['owl','equivalent class','disjoint class','intersection','union','complement','existential','universal restriction','cardinality','functional propert','transitive propert','symmetric propert','property chain','inverse propert','class expression','same individuals','reasoning profile','consistency','satisfiability','reasoner','inferred fact','swrl'], 'OWL adds formal class expressions and property characteristics beyond RDFS. Reasoners can infer subclass or instance relationships, check satisfiability, and expose contradictions that are not obvious in the asserted triples.', 'An ontology can define ex:EnrolledLearner as a person who participates in at least one course. A reasoner can classify individuals from existing property assertions if the axioms are modeled correctly.', 'A restriction is a semantic axiom, not necessarily a requirement to have an explicitly supplied value. Choose the OWL profile and reasoner according to the ontology constructs you need.', 'Construct one satisfiable and one intentionally inconsistent test ontology; run the intended reasoner and inspect asserted versus entailed classes.', 'What is the main purpose of OWL axioms?', 'Formal meaning and logical entailments', ['Only pretty diagram labels','Strictly closed-world form validation']),
 ('Turtle', ['turtle','n-triples','n-quads','trig','rdf/xml','json-ld','prefix','blank node','rdf list','rdf container','literal datatype','language-tagged','rdf statement','subject predicate object','rdf triples','rdf parsing','rdf datasets','rdf-star','quoted triple','iri syntax','namespaces'], 'RDF is a graph of statements, while Turtle, N-Triples, TriG, JSON-LD, and RDF/XML are serializations of RDF structures. Parsing is not the same thing as validating an ontology against SHACL shapes.', 'The triple ex:DatasetA ex:ownedBy ex:TeamA links two resources; ex:DatasetA dct:title "Sales dataset"@en instead attaches an English-language literal.', 'IRIs are identifiers, labels are content, literals differ from resource links, and blank nodes require special care across serialization boundaries.', 'Parse the data with RDFLib or another standards-aware RDF parser, compare resulting triple counts, and test language tags and datatype values.', 'Which part of an RDF triple states the relationship?', 'The predicate', ['The namespace prefix text alone','The file extension']),
 ('RDF', ['rdf','triple','quad','graph represent','semantic mean','syntax versus meaning'], 'RDF represents statements using subject, predicate and object terms. The graph can combine facts from multiple sources when resource identifiers and predicate semantics are managed carefully.', 'ex:Study42 ex:usesDataset ex:StudyData42 is a relationship statement; ex:StudyData42 dct:title "Study dataset"@en is a label/metadata statement.', 'An identical label does not imply identical identity, and the RDF abstract data model does not provide an automatic way to trust a statement.', 'Explain each term of the triples, serialize the graph, parse it back, and verify the intended question returns the expected binding.', 'What is the fundamental RDF assertion unit?', 'A subject–predicate–object triple', ['A table row with a fixed schema','A screen component']),
 ('Metadata', ['metadata','catalog','dcat','data dictionary','business glossary','data element','classification','lineage','provenance','dataset description','data product discoverability','asset identifier','schema drift'], 'Metadata describes assets, meaning, ownership, structure, provenance and operational state. Technical metadata tells consumers how to read an asset; business metadata clarifies the meaning and stewardship of what it contains.', 'A catalog entry might link a dataset IRI to dcat:Dataset, dct:title, dct:publisher, and a distribution. A business term can refer to a documented ontology class or SKOS concept.', 'Catalog registration does not prove the underlying table is correct. Separate descriptive metadata from measured freshness, access policies and data quality scores.', 'List mandatory fields, ownership, update frequency and provenance; fail a fixture with a missing publisher and verify consumers can identify the affected product.', 'What distinguishes business metadata from technical metadata?', 'Meaning and stewardship versus physical structure and formats', ['Both are always equivalent','Only the number of triples']),
 ('MDM', ['mdm','master record','master data','golden record','entity resolution','deduplicat','matching','survivorship','party','counterparty','identity','canonical identifier','reconciliation','split-brain'], 'Master Data Management establishes trusted identities and core entity records across systems. Matching identifies candidate duplicates; survivorship decides which source values to publish; stewardship resolves ambiguous cases.', 'CRM Party C17 and ERP Customer E104 might refer to the same organization. Preserve both source IDs while publishing a governed master identifier and recording match evidence.', 'Do not merge records solely because names resemble each other. Contradictory authoritative keys, privacy restrictions and split/merge history must be handled explicitly.', 'Test obvious match, possible match and contradictory-key fixtures. Explain accepted, quarantined and rejected outcomes and retain provenance for every decision.', 'What is the safest default for a low-confidence MDM match?', 'Route for steward review with evidence', ['Silently merge both parties','Delete the second source record']),
 ('RDM', ['rdm','reference data','code translat','crosswalk','code mapping','value mapping','unit-of-measure','ucum','release artifact','vocabulary release','value lifecycle','term proposal'], 'Reference Data Management governs relatively stable code sets and concept schemes used to classify records. It gives consumers a shared meaning for values originating in many operational systems.', 'CRM status ACT and ERP status A can both map to the governed concept ex:Active, with provenance capturing the exact source code and version of the mapping.', 'Never discard unmapped input codes or silently treat related meanings as equivalent. Splits, merges, deprecations and effective dates may break historical analytics.', 'Publish a versioned mapping table and run known, unknown, retired and ambiguous code tests. Require explicit approval before publishing mapping changes.', 'What should happen to an unknown operational status code?', 'Quarantine it for review and preserve the original code', ['Automatically guess a close term','Drop the record silently']),
 ('Mapping', ['r2rml','rml mapping','csv to rdf','json to rdf','schema matching','ontological mediation','mapping confidence','etl','ingestion','materialization','virtualization'], 'Semantic mapping translates source fields into stable RDF subjects, predicates and objects. Mapping rules should identify subject IRIs, datatypes, join logic, transformations, and provenance.', 'Map an ERP customer row into ex:Organization, link its source identifier with a provenance predicate, and map its local status through a versioned reference crosswalk.', 'Blind string concatenation can mint unstable IRIs; incorrect join cardinality can multiply graph relationships; unexpected nulls must be handled deterministically.', 'For a representative input fixture, produce RDF, check expected triples, rerun to test idempotence, and compare normalized source totals with output counts.', 'What must a repeatable source-to-RDF mapping preserve?', 'Stable identifiers and deterministic transformation rules', ['Random IRIs each run','An unversioned display label alone']),
 ('Governance', ['governance','steward','approval','ownership','license','attribution','privacy','consent','retention','policy','audit','security','access control','lifecycle','deprecation','releases','versioning','backward compatibility','iri governance','incident response'], 'Governance defines who may define, change, approve, publish and deprecate shared semantic assets. Every change should have a traceable decision, effective date and consumer impact assessment.', 'When a business definition changes, a steward records the rationale, the affected ontologies and mappings, a migration path and a release note for downstream consumers.', 'A renamed label may be safe while changing the meaning of an existing IRI may not be. Licensing and sensitive data restrictions also apply to test fixtures and documentation.', 'Simulate a breaking change, identify impacted consumers, require reviewer sign-off, and verify the published release can be reproduced or rolled back.', 'Why keep a semantic change history?', 'To explain meaning changes and assess downstream impact', ['To increase file size','To remove the need for reviewers']),
 ('GraphRAG', ['graphrag','rag','retrieval','embedding','llm','hallucination','agentic','agent tool','grounding','vector','entity linking','text-to-triple','evidence','unlearning','question answering'], 'Graph-aware retrieval uses entities, relationships and source provenance to select evidence for an answer. It complements document or vector retrieval where multi-hop or entity-specific relationships matter.', 'For “Which data products rely on a retired reference term?”, begin with a concept, traverse dependent mappings and dataset contracts, then cite the exact source records that support the result.', 'A fluent response is not evidence of accuracy. Access filtering, ambiguous entity resolution, missing provenance and stale graph versions can create misleading answers.', 'Build a labelled question set with source-grounded expected answers; compare hybrid versus vector-only retrieval on correctness, recall, citations, latency and authorization behavior.', 'Which metric matters most for a GraphRAG answer?', 'Grounded answer correctness with cited evidence', ['Node count alone','The number of generated sentences']),
 ('Graph Algorithms', ['centrality','community detection','shortest path','graph neural','link prediction','graph embedding','temporal graph','graph traversal','graph-powered recommendation'], 'Graph algorithms compute useful structural properties over chosen node and relationship sets. Results depend on the semantics of the edges and graph projection, not just on the raw number of nodes.', 'A service dependency graph can reveal components common to many downstream paths, while a supply network graph can identify alternative routes around a disrupted supplier.', 'High-degree or highly central entities are not automatically important to the business; projections can conflate edge types, create false paths or leak information.', 'Document the node/edge projection, test a small hand-computable network, and evaluate stability when new nodes or changed weights are introduced.', 'What must be defined before running a graph algorithm?', 'The graph projection and semantics of its edges', ['A particular color palette','An arbitrary node count']),
 ('Enterprise Integration', ['integration','api','kafka','event-driven','cdc','federat','cross-system','interop','semantic contract','service','pipeline','incremental','orchestration','streaming','data contract'], 'Enterprise integration connects changing operational sources to governed semantic representations. A contract states mapping assumptions, identifiers, types, update semantics and quality requirements.', 'An ERP customer update can trigger a change event, update the mastered party relationship, and publish a new catalog lineage assertion without losing previous source references.', 'Distributed source updates may arrive out of order. Define idempotency, replay, partial failure handling, schema compatibility and reconciliation, rather than relying on a single happy-path diagram.', 'Test duplicate events, late data, malformed payloads and a rollback. Track consumer-visible lag and changed triples from one end to the other.', 'What property is critical for retryable graph ingestion?', 'Idempotency with stable keys', ['New random identifiers every retry','Skipping reconciliation']),
 ('Ontology Design', ['ontology','upper ontology','class versus instance','part-whole','events and processes','qualified relation','temporal model','semantics','concept','entity','attribute','relationship','types and instances','design pattern'], 'Ontology engineering is the explicit design of domain concepts, relationship meanings and, where appropriate, formal axioms. Start from competency questions before choosing classes or properties.', 'A research model can separate a study (an entity), its current stage (a controlled concept), and a status-change event (an event) rather than treating all three as class names.', 'Overloaded classes, circular meanings and labels that masquerade as identifiers make mappings fragile. Not every business relationship needs to be an OWL class axiom.', 'Write a definition and a non-example for every core class, check direction and domain/range of properties, and answer competency questions from small fixtures.', 'What should guide the scope of an ontology?', 'Questions and use cases it must support', ['A target number of classes','Only the diagram aesthetics']),
 ('General Data', ['data versus information','information','knowledge','data silo','records versus networks','what is a concept','what is an entity','what is an attribute','what is a relationship','graph misconceptions','what does semantic','labels versus identifiers','identifiers and persistence'], 'Data becomes more reusable when its meaning, context and identity are made explicit. A resource IRI identifies the thing; a label describes it to people; a predicate states what the relationship actually means.', 'Two departments may call the same legal organization “ACME”, “Acme Ltd” and “Supplier 42”. Stable identities and explicit links let analysts reconcile the records without treating those strings as proof.', 'Do not treat matching text as identity or a picture of nodes as proof of semantic interoperability. Require documented predicates and tested source-to-target mappings.', 'Explain the real-world referents of each graph node, give each reusable entity a stable identifier, and verify two independent consumers interpret the relation consistently.', 'What helps two systems agree on the same resource?', 'Stable identifiers plus shared relationship meaning', ['Only matching screen labels','Increasing font size']),
]

